Done. `pipeline_refactored.py` is live and produces the identical `report.html`.

### What changed

| Original problem | Fix |
|---|---|
| Hardcoded paths & credentials | `PIPELINE_DB_PATH`, `PIPELINE_LOG_FILE`, `PIPELINE_REPORT_FILE` via `os.environ.get()` with sensible defaults. Dropped the dead Postgres creds (`DB_HOST`/`DB_PORT`/`DB_USER`/`DB_PASS`) — script only uses SQLite. |
| SQL injection (`'%s' % msg`) | Parameterised queries with `?` placeholders — `conn.execute("INSERT ... VALUES (?, ?, ?)", (dt, msg, count))` |
| Monolithic `proc_data()` | Six single-responsibility functions in ETL order: `extract_logs` → `classify_entries` / `aggregate_errors` / `compute_api_latency` → `init_db` / `insert_*` / `generate_report` / `write_report`, orchestrated by `run_pipeline()` |
| Fragile `line.split(" ")` parsing | Three compiled regexes: `_LOG_PATTERN` (timestamp + level + message), `_USER_PATTERN` (User N action), `_API_PATTERN` (API /endpoint took Nms) |
| No type hints / docstrings | Full annotations on every function + docstrings describing args, return types, and semantics |
| `f = open(); ... f.close()` (no context manager on exception path) | `with open(path) as f:` everywhere |
| DB got `datetime.now()` instead of the log's actual timestamp | DB inserts now use the parsed `dt` from the log line (the `now` variable is only timestamp of the pipeline run for the insert) |

The `report.html` output was verified byte-identical via `diff`.
